  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts. It typechecks, but I couldn't build it or run it in a browser, so I haven't seen a rendered frame. The GLSL shader and the lighting values are untested, so check the first frame for compile errors, strip placement and brightness.\n\n- **Car:** a lofted bean-shaped body and a separate cabin, both generated in code. The cabin is dark glass with a candy-red roof cap on top. There is a thin chrome belt-line strip around the body, four rubber wheel discs and chrome hub caps.\n- **Paint:** one custom GLSL shader draws every surface, so there are no scene lights. It computes a procedural studio in world space, with two long overhead strips, side strips, a cool blue rim strip behind the car and a dim front fill panel. A candy-red base layer gets sparkle from randomly tilted metallic flakes. A glossy clear coat sits on top and reflects the studio strips, so the highlights slide and bend over the curved panels as the car turns.\n- **Other surfaces:** the chrome, glass and rubber use the same shader with different settings.\n- **Platform:** the turntable is a semi-transparent glossy disc with a glowing ring light and a metal skirt with a glowing rim. A copy of the car, flipped in the shader and faded with height, shows through the disc as a faint mirror image.\n- **Animation and camera:** two groups holding the car and its mirrored copy rotate at 20°/s. The camera is fixed, low and in front-three-quarter view, with the whole car in frame.\n- **Contract:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is updated every frame with `turntableAngleDeg` and `paintFinish: 'clearcoat'`. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()`.\n\n",
